  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Cuphophyllus subviolaceus is a small waxcap mushroom characterized by its delicate, waxy appearance and subtle coloration. It is part of the diverse Hygrocybaceae family, known for their distinctive translucent gills and fragile fruiting bodies.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Waxy Gill Texture",
      "description": "The gills are distinctly waxy or slippery in appearance, a hallmark of the waxcap family",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Violet-Tinged Cap",
      "description": "The cap displays subtle violet or purplish hues, especially toward the center, which distinguishes it from related species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Size",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are typically small and delicate, rarely exceeding 2-3 cm in diameter",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species is found in moist, grassy areas and meadows throughout New England, preferring nutrient-poor soils and mossy substrates.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Waxcaps like C. subviolaceus are considered indicators of ancient grasslands and unimproved pastures, making them valuable for assessing ecosystem health and conservation value.",
